  "turn": {
    "pericope": "Matthew 20:1-16",
    "refKey": "Matt.20.1-Matt.20.16",
    "gravity": "The day pairs Jonah — furious on his hill that God spared Nineveh — with the Psalm's slow-to-anger mercy and the vineyard's equal denarius. One nerve runs through all three: God's generosity offends the deserving. Grace is not fair, and the longest-serving resent it most.",
    "trap": "It becomes a lesson about fairness (and how the workers should have read the fine print), or a warm word that \"God rewards everyone\" — either way the scandal is explained off. The parable's offense is precisely the equal wage; defuse it and there is no sermon left.",
    "hinge": "The householder does no one wrong — every promise is kept — and the first are still furious. Grace that is genuinely unfair to our accounting. The only question the text leaves open is whether you can rejoice in that or only resent it.",
    "doors": [
      {
        "landing": "One denarius for all",
        "claim": "The wage is eternal life itself, and it is given whole to everyone — heaven is not a pay scale, and the latecomer is not shortchanged.",
        "witnesses": [
          {
            "author": "Origen",
            "tradition": "greek-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "The denarius here I take to mean salvation itself."
          },
          {
            "author": "Augustine",
            "tradition": "latin-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "Because life eternal shall be equal to all the saints, one denarius is given to all — though in that life the light of merits will still shine diversely."
          },
          {
            "author": "Jerome",
            "tradition": "latin-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "The denarius bears the figure of the King: you have received my image and likeness. What more do you desire?"
          }
        ],
        "say": "What you are paid is God's own life, given to you entire — there is no larger wage to envy.",
        "do": "Lead them to receive the denarius as gift, not earnings — to name where they have been counting their hours instead of marvelling at the coin."
      },
      {
        "landing": "The eleventh hour",
        "claim": "The hours are the ages of the world; the last hired are the latecomers — the Gentiles, the deathbed convert, anyone called after us — and they are seated as our equals.",
        "witnesses": [
          {
            "author": "Gregory the Great",
            "tradition": "latin-patristic",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "The morning is the age from Adam to Noah, the third hour Noah to Abraham, the sixth and ninth to Moses and to the Lord, and the eleventh hour from the Lord's coming to the end of the world."
          },
          {
            "author": "John Wesley",
            "tradition": "wesleyan",
            "work": "Explanatory Notes",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "The eleventh hour: the Gentiles, called long after the Jews into the vineyard of the Church — or those in any age who were called late."
          }
        ],
        "say": "You may have worked since morning, but the kingdom is not seniority — the one called at the eleventh hour sits beside you, equal.",
        "do": "Turn long-time members from a claim of priority to gladness at the latecomer — one person they would secretly rank below themselves."
      },
      {
        "landing": "The evil eye",
        "claim": "The laborers were not cheated of anything; their grievance is simply that others got more than they deserved — and that envy of grace is the sin the parable exposes in us.",
        "witnesses": [
          {
            "author": "Remigius of Auxerre",
            "tradition": "medieval",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "By the eye is meant the purpose: they had an evil eye, an evil purpose, because they were grieved at the salvation of the others."
          },
          {
            "author": "Pseudo-Chrysostom",
            "tradition": "pseudonymous",
            "work": "Catena Aurea",
            "mode": "text",
            "reading": "Their complaint was not that they were defrauded of their wage, but that the others received more than they deserved — for the envious have as much pain at another's good as at their own ill."
          }
        ],
        "say": "Search the resentment in your own chest when grace runs to someone who didn't earn it — that evil eye, not the wage, is the danger here.",
        "do": "Send them, like Jonah left outside the city, to one place they are angry that God has been merciful, and to repent of the grudge."
      }
    ],
    "subtract": "Three doors, three landings — wonder at the equal coin, humility before the latecomer, repentance of the evil eye. They cannot all be preached at once; pick the one your people most need and let the other two go.",
